Consider the problem of modeling the interiors of ice-giant planets having masses and radii comparable to Uranus (mass ≈ 14.5 M_⊕, radius ≈ 25 559 km) and Neptune (mass ≈ 17.1 M_⊕, radius ≈ 24 622 km), under conditions of pressures up to several hundred GPa and temperatures up to several thousand K characteristic of their deep interiors. Let the candidate constituents be: water (H₂O), ammonia (NH₃), methane (CH₄), rock (a silicate/iron component), hydrogen (H₂), and helium (He). For each pure component i denote by P_i(ρ,T) its equation of state (EOS) relating pressure, density and temperature, and for any mixture with mass fractions {x_i} denote by P_mix(ρ,T,{x_i}) the corresponding mixture EOS, including possible non-ideal corrections, phase boundaries (fluid, superionic, plasma, demixing), and chemical reactions among components.

Determine, with quantitative accuracy sufficient to constrain interior structure models that match the observed mass, radius, gravitational moments J₂, J₄, and rotation rate of Uranus and Neptune:

(a) the pure-component high-pressure–temperature EOSs P_i(ρ,T) for H₂O, NH₃, CH₄, rock, H₂, and He throughout the relevant (P,T) domain;
(b) the mixing rules / non-ideal mixture EOSs P_mix for the binary and multi-component mixtures (H₂O+NH₃+CH₄), (ices+rock), and (ices+H₂+He), including miscibility gaps and conditions for demixing or differentiation;
(c) the chemical interactions and phase transitions among these species (e.g. dissociation, ionization, superionic phases, formation of new compounds) that occur in the relevant (P,T) regime;

so that interior structure models built from this input can rigorously exclude compositional profiles inconsistent with the observed bulk and gravitational data, and can determine which materials are mixed versus differentiated within the planets.

